    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/0/50690.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 10,
    "turn_text": "Yes, and look, I'm sharing a lot of input on what is the pros and what are the pros and cons of one versus two reconciliation bills because I do think there's some pitfalls with the two bill approach, but I've laid out what those are. You know, ultimately the president is going to decide. So, you know, I made it clear, you know, with the people who are going to be involved in making this decision, what I think, but also what are the pros and cons so that ultimately the president makes the best decision because look, you at the end of the day, we're all in agreement of what will be in this package. One package, two packages, we're going to do border security. We're going to do energy security. We're going to do tax reform. We're going to do rules and regulations or reform. So it's going to be things that are great for the American people, great for working families to lower costs, to get our country back on track, to give the President the tools he needs to secure the border, and to prevent a $4.5 trillion tax increase. I'm committed to doing all of those things. What is the best method for Congress to get it all done? For most Americans, they don't care. Just go get it done. So our focus needs to be on making this decision You know, we're going to start in January. We're going to hit the ground running. We're already working on the policy, the legislation, you know, to get some of this stuff done starting in January. But we've got to have this final call made. The president himself has been, you know, of course, lobbied, so to speak, on both approaches. But he's kept his powder dry. But I think pretty soon he's going to make a decision. And once he makes a decision, let's all go in that direction. But the policy is something we don't disagree on. We're all in sync on the same page of what needs to be done to get our country back on track when",
